  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe deepening area of low pressure off the coast will combine \nwith a surface high pressure over the Great Lakes to produce \nincreasing northeast winds over central NY and northeast PA \nbeginning early this morning, and continuing through the \nweekend. This system is expected to take a meandering track to \nthe north today and hug the NJ coast Sunday before moving \nnortheastward across New England on Monday.\n\nAs this system has begun to take shape, confidence has\nincreased slightly that rainfall will spread slightly more\nwestward and inland than anticipated on Sunday. Though we're\nseeing a push more westward, the threat for flooding or\nhydrological issues still remains low. With the bulk of expected\nto fall on Sunday, overall rainfall amounts beginning this\nmorning through Sunday night is a half to three-quarter inch for\nnortheast PA, Catskills, and portions of the Southern Tier, and\nless than a half inch elsewhere.\n\nMeanwhile, wind gusts should top out around 20 to 30 mph for \nmost of the populated areas of central NY and northeast PA, but\nsome of the higher elevations could see occasional 30 to 40 mph\nwind gusts through the weekend. \n\nThe event winds down later on Monday/Mon night as the low picks\nup speed and lifts to the northeast. Rain will come to an end \nMonday night and winds will subside. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA weak ridge of high pressure builds in across the Northeast US\nTuesday and Wednesday, which will cause large scale suppression\nand usher in a gradually drying air mass. Skies should clear \nout Tuesday night and continue into Wednesday. A warmer air mass\nmoving in combined with plenty of sun on Wed will allow \ntemperatures to climb into the lower to mid 70s. \n\nThere does appear to be another open short wave that attempts \nto move in from the west Thursday or Friday, but the timing and\nplacement of the boundary is still in question. If the wave \nholds back to the west, temperatures could climb again into the \n70s. However if more clouds and rain move in quicker, \ntemperatures could be substantially cooler.",
